title: 'pallet-assets: mirror balance changes as ERC-20 Transfer logs via AssetsCallback'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `AssetsCallback` gains infallible balance-change hooks (`issued`, `transferred`, `burned`,
    `deposited`, `withdrawn`) fired next to the matching pallet-assets events on every path:
    extrinsics, the `fungibles` traits (incl. XCM asset transactors) and the `Balanced` imbalance
    paths. `fungibles::Mutate::shelve` and `restore` have no pallet-assets event of their own and
    fire `burned` and `issued`, since they move total supply like a burn and a mint do; likewise
    the dust a transfer burns (`TransferFlags::burn_dust`) fires `burned` with no event of its
    own. The trait gains a third generic parameter `Balance`; any hand-written
    `impl AssetsCallback<Id, Acct>` must add it. Genesis asset creation now fires `created` too, so
    a `CallbackHandle` seeds genesis assets like runtime-created ones; a failure there panics
    genesis construction.

    On a runtime wiring a log-mirroring `CallbackHandle`, every balance-changing operation deposits
    an additional `pallet_revive::Event::ContractEmitted` right after the pallet-assets event, so
    per-extrinsic event count and ordering change for substrate-side consumers. Code asserting on
    the last emitted event (e.g. `assert_last_event`) must assert presence instead.

    `pallet_revive::Pallet::emit_contract_log_outside_frame` is exposed for runtime components that
    mirror non-contract activity as EVM logs from outside any contract call frame, with a matching
    `Tracing::log_event_outside_frame` hook so tracing such a log no longer panics. The in-frame
    `log_event` (the `LOG` opcode) keeps its active-frame invariant as a debug assertion.

    A new `Erc20TransferLogsCallback` in `pallet-assets-precompiles` mirrors the balance changes as
    canonical ERC-20 `Transfer` logs at the asset's precompile address. The `ERC20` precompile no
    longer emits its own `Transfer` log for a non-zero transfer, so wire this callback as the
    instance's `CallbackHandle` to keep precompile transfers logged; a zero-value transfer moves
    no balance and stays self-emitted, as ERC-20 requires. Asset Hub Westend wires the callback
    for the trust-backed and pool instances (inline-id) and for foreign assets (via
    `ForeignIdConfig` and the asset-index map). The kitchensink runtime wires only the
    foreign-asset index-map callback and no mirror, so its non-zero precompile transfers emit no
    `Transfer` log.

    A log emitted with no ethereum transaction open — a mirror from a plain extrinsic or an XCM
    balance change — has no receipt to attach to, so it is buffered per block and flushed in
    `on_finalize` as one synthetic transaction, entering the block's `logs_bloom`, `receipts_root`
    and transaction trie. Such a block therefore holds one more transaction than it has real
    ethereum transactions; eth-rpc rebuilds the same payload from block number and chain id to
    recover its hash and serves those logs against it. `eth_receipt_data_versioned` is bumped to
    version 2, reporting that transaction separately: its gas entry plus the event index of each
    log the block committed to it. A consumer rebuilding those logs from block events must select
    by those indices, since the block's `ContractEmitted` events are a superset of what the header
    commits to, and number them after every other log of the block: eth-rpc gives them a
    `logIndex` from the block's event count on, and `eth_getLogs` now orders its results by block
    number and `logIndex`, so the synthetic transaction's logs come last as its `transactionIndex`
    is. Before, the order of a filtered query's results was unspecified. `eth_block` lists the
    synthetic transaction's hash in every version, since
    `transactions` is the list the header commits to; an eth-rpc on receipt data version 1 sees
    that hash with no receipt to serve for it. The new `Config` item `MaxOutsideFrameLogs` bounds
    the buffer as a backstop; a log arriving past the cap is not buffered, so the bloom omits it
    while its `ContractEmitted` still stands. Zero turns the buffer off, leaving such logs
    substrate-only as they were before.

    Contract logs emitted outside an ethereum transaction (`Revive::call`, an XCM `Transact` into
    a contract) do not use the buffer and stay substrate-only, as before. Only pallet mirrors and
    the precompile's zero-value `Transfer` do, so every buffered log is priced at the weight of
    the operation that produced it: a pallet-assets `transfer()` or `mint()`, a precompile
    transfer driven from a contract, or the hold operation that settles a contract's storage
    deposit, which on Asset Hub Westend is mirrored once per contract whose net deposit changed
    in the call and is the highest-volume producer once the buffer is on. The cheap route into
    the buffer, a raw `LOG` at a few hundred gas, is closed.

    The ethereum receipt now follows storage rollback. What a transaction has committed to its
    receipt is kept in storage while it runs, so a log is dropped from the receipt together with
    the state change that produced it, whichever layer rolled back: a contract frame that
    reverted, a `batch_all`, `proxy` or `force_batch` around a plain call whose balance change was
    mirrored, or the whole dispatch failing after its frames at fee settlement, which commits a
    receipt with no logs and an empty bloom. Before, such logs stayed in the receipt,
    `receipts_root` and `logs_bloom` while the state and events that produced them rolled back.
    `seal_deposit_event` is now benchmarked inside an ethereum transaction, where the log is
    RLP-encoded and accrued into the receipt's bloom. Every `eth_transact` always did that work,
    but the benchmark emitted its log outside a transaction and never measured it. Its fixed part
    doubles (5.7 to 12.3 µs on dev, 6.9 to 15.0 µs on Asset Hub Westend) and its per-byte part
    rises by 16% on dev and 53% on Asset Hub Westend; the storage round trip for the committed
    state is a small share of that. Every gas estimate for a log-emitting call rises with it.

    The cap is a backstop no block can reach. Buffering a log registers at least its encoded bytes
    as proof size, unchecked, whatever produced it — an assets extrinsic, a storage deposit settled
    by a contract frame, an XCM fee swap, a batch. That is an admission charge rather than a cost:
    the buffer is written and taken within the block, so the drain reads it from the overlay and
    the proof holds one absence lookup for the key, nothing per entry. The charge binds all the
    same, since it lands in `BlockWeight` and proof-size reclaim only swaps out an extrinsic's own
    weight. The smallest entry, an address with no topics and no data, is 26 bytes, so Asset Hub
    Westend's 10 MiB proof budget admits about 403,000 of them against the 524,288 it wires once
    enabled; its runtime tests pin that bound against the value. The bound is not exact: an
    unchecked registration can overshoot `max_block` from `on_initialize` and from a block's last
    extrinsic, and ref_time runs out long before either. A runtime with an unbounded proof budget
    has no such bound and should size its cap from ref_time, or leave the buffer off.

    Asset Hub Westend ships with the buffer off (`MaxOutsideFrameLogs = 0`): a mirrored balance
    change with no ethereum transaction open is a `ContractEmitted` event and nothing else, so a
    block's transaction list is what any eth-rpc can serve. Receipts of new blocks still change in
    two ways. Logs of reverted frames and of failed transactions leave the receipt, `receipts_root`
    and `logs_bloom`. An asset balance change dispatched through `eth_substrate_call` now puts its
    mirrored `Transfer` on the ethereum receipt. Past blocks are unaffected and no migration is
    needed. Turning the buffer on is a separate runtime upgrade, wiring
    `OUTSIDE_FRAME_LOGS_CAP_ONCE_ENABLED`, after every eth-rpc serving the chain reads receipt
    data version 2.

    Follow-up: a buffered log's share of the `on_finalize` drain is registered on the block rather
    than charged to the emitter, which is why contract frames are kept out of the buffer. A
    follow-up PR charges the drain to the emitting frame's weight and lets contract logs in.

    Rollout to a live Asset Hub (e.g. Polkadot) needs care beyond wiring the callback:
      - Atomic cutover: removing the precompile self-emit and wiring the callback must ship in the
        same runtime upgrade, else a block range emits zero or duplicate `Transfer` logs.
      - eth-rpc goes first. Release the eth-rpc that reads receipt data version 2, announce it as
        the minimum version, allow an upgrade window, then enable the buffer in a runtime upgrade
        of its own. Runtime and eth-rpc upgrade independently before that point: with the buffer
        off, both versions list one hash per receipt entry. After the buffer is on, an eth-rpc on
        version 1 is unsupported: for a block with mirrored logs it lists the synthetic
        transaction's hash in `eth_getBlockByNumber` but returns null for its receipt and
        transaction, reports one fewer in `eth_getBlockTransactionCountByNumber`, and serves none
        of the mirrored logs. Its receipt indexing does not break, since version 1 of the receipt
        data has one entry per ethereum transaction as before.
      - Weights: the mirror's cost lands in the weights of every pallet that moves assets, so a
        runtime wiring it must re-run `/cmd bench` for those pallets, not only for pallet-assets.
        That includes the XCM fee instructions where a swap trader moves assets, once their
        benchmark exercises the swap; Asset Hub Westend's does in a separate PR. The benchmarks
        measure against the configured cap, so the buffer's append is in those weights only once
        they ran with it on; the upgrade that turns the buffer on re-runs them.
      - Foreign asset-index mappings must exist before a mirror can resolve a token address. The
        creation callback establishes them, genesis assets included; assets predating the callback
        wiring need the index-map migration to backfill them.
      - Asset destruction has no mirror: ERC-20 has no equivalent concept, and firing a burn log
        per holder would emit an unbounded number of events for one destruction. Log consumers
        must drop a token's balances once the asset is destroyed, or an inline id later reused by
        `force_create` will inherit them.
      - Genesis-seeded balances have no mirror either: genesis creation fires `created`, but the
        accounts it endows are credited with no `Issued` event or `issued`/`deposited` callback.
        Log consumers must seed genesis balances from storage and apply logs on top.
      - Same-account holds are kept out of the projection: `ERC20::balanceOf` now reports
        `fungibles::Inspect::total_balance` (free plus held), so a hold or release moves balance
        within one account without moving `balanceOf` and needs no log. The hold paths that move
        value between accounts — `transfer_on_hold`, `transfer_and_hold`, `burn_held` — do move
        it, and run through pallet-assets' `Unbalanced` impl, below the `Mutate` impl the balance
        hooks hang off. `pallet-assets-holder` therefore reports them itself, firing `transferred`
        and `burned` from `done_transfer_on_hold` and `done_burn_held`; on Asset Hub Westend these
        are the paths `pallet_revive`'s `PGasDeposit` drives for every contract storage deposit
        and refund paid in PGAS (a payer without PGAS pays in the native asset, with no mirror).
        A runtime pairing the mirror with a different `Holder` implementation must report
        the same three paths, or balance moves through them with no `Transfer` log.
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    Upgrade eth-rpc before the chain turns the outside-of-frame log buffer on. This release reads
    receipt data version 2, which carries the synthetic transaction holding a block's mirrored
    logs. An older eth-rpc on a chain with the buffer on lists that transaction's hash in a block
    but returns null for its receipt and transaction, reports one fewer in
    `eth_getBlockTransactionCountByNumber` and serves none of the mirrored logs. With the buffer
    off, as Asset Hub Westend ships it, old and new eth-rpc serve the same blocks. `eth_getLogs`
    now returns logs ordered by block number and `logIndex`; before, the order of a filtered
    query's results was unspecified. No database migration.
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-revive-eth-rpc`: `ReceiptExtractor::new` and `new_with_custom_address_recovery` take
    the chain's EVM chain id as a second argument, which the extractor needs to rebuild the
    synthetic transaction that carries a block's outside-of-frame logs, and are plain functions
    now, neither `async` nor fallible.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    Ethereum receipts no longer carry the logs of reverted frames or of failed transactions;
    they, `receipts_root` and `logs_bloom` now follow the state that produced the logs. Gas
    estimates for calls that emit logs go up, since a `LOG` is now priced as it runs inside an
    ethereum transaction. `eth_getLogs` returns logs ordered by block number and `logIndex`. On
    a chain wiring the mirror, such as Asset Hub Westend, an asset balance change dispatched
    through `eth_substrate_call` shows up as an ERC-20 `Transfer` log on its receipt.
crates:
- name: pallet-assets
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-revive-types
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: major
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: major
- name: pallet-assets-holder
  bump: major
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: major
- name: penpal-runtime
  bump: minor
